fix(desktop): TBAR describes exactly the server the page shows

Regression-first (FUNDAMENTAL CONTRACT suite in
desktop-lifecycle.test.ts): in low-memory single-tab mode the
origin-to-view map moves at the click while the retained page keeps
rendering the old server until it has painted the new one. A poll tick
landing in that window handed the OLD server's CPU/activity/workspace —
and via the identity catch-up even its hostname and accent — to the
newly selected profile's taskbar bar, header tier and rail entry. One
server is never another server; that is a defect, never a trade-off.

Attribution now follows the page, the same authority the routing
pointer already follows:

- The page commits the profile it has painted (__phiEmbed.current(),
  web-src/util.ts commitEmbeddedProfile): seeded at boot from the URL
  the host committed to, and committed in _performSwitch only after
  paintBackendFromStore, so it is the DOM's truth, not the click's
  intent. The poll script and observeProfileIdentity read it first and
  drop any reading whose reported profile does not match the polled
  profile; a page with no identity (legacy surface, boot still loading)
  reads through the origin-to-view map, which is exact for dedicated
  views.
- The taskbar bar shows every reading it is given: the >50% gate —
  untouched since the original desktop commit, so never a tuned
  decision — hid the bar for most real loads, which reads as a broken
  bar. The bar is now cpu/100 whenever a finite reading exists.
- Restoring the window re-polls immediately: a hidden/minimized window
  keeps its last reading by design, so on restore that reading is
  stale; the restore event now samples instead of letting the bar
  describe the pre-minimize server for up to a full 10s tick.

The identity-read mechanics tests (read counts, slot ordering) were
updated for the gate probe that now precedes every identity DOM read;
the >50 threshold test now pins the describe-everything rule.
